===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)kreybʰ-}}
From {{der|en|la|circumscrībō}}, from {{m|la|circum|t=around}} + {{m|la|scrībō|t=write}}. Surface analysis: {{af|en|circum-|scribe}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɜː.kəm.skɹaɪb/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɝ.kəm.skɹaɪb/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-circumscribe.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-circumscribe.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪb|s=3}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[draw]] a line [[around]]; to [[encircle]].
# To [[limit]] narrowly; to [[restrict]].
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|page=29|volume=III|passage="Well, I promise you to '''circumscribe''' her conquests as much as possible by extending my own," returned Henrietta. "It will be an easy task; for Miss Churchill does not do 'the honours of her eyes.' I often tell her her beauty is quite wasted upon her."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-07|author=David Simpson
|volume=188|issue=26|page=36|magazine={{w|The Guardian Weekly}}
|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/books/2013/may/18/falling-upwards-richard-holmes-review Fantasy of navigation]
|passage=It is tempting to speculate about the incentives or compulsions that might explain why anyone would take to the skies in [the] basket [of a balloon]:&nbsp;[&hellip;]; perhaps to moralise on the oneness or fragility of the planet, or to see humanity for the small and '''circumscribed''' thing that it is;&nbsp;[&hellip;].}}
# {{lb|en|geometry}} To draw the [[small]]est [[circle]] or higher-dimensional [[sphere]] that has (a polyhedron, polygon, etc.) in its interior.

